<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikkō-ikki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ikko-ikki, Ikko leagues</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ikkō-ikki were leagues of farmers, local warriors, townspeople and priests belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in 15th- and 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1488 they overthrew the governor of Kaga and ruled the province until 1580, and from the fortified Ishiyama Hongan-ji they resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> for a decade before surrendering in 1580.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki.jpg" alt="Ikkō-ikki" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turumaru_Flags.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
